\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Rotation degree and continuum helicity}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For a massless particle of fixed standard momentum, the
continuum little group acts on its states. In the finite-helicity
class considered by Weinberg, its abelian translation subgroup
acts trivially. In #{4} spacetime dimensions the helicity
#{\lambda} labels the state's representation of the remaining
rotations. Weinberg labels an irreducible finite-dimensional
Lorentz field representation by #{(A,B)}, assigning the positive
imaginary boost term to the first factor and the negative one
to the second. Suppose a field transforms in this representation
and one of its components has a nonzero vacuum-to-one-particle
matrix element. Then that particle must have #{\lambda=B-A}.
Under this condition type #{(1/2,0)} requires helicity #{-1/2},
and type #{(0,1/2)} requires #{+1/2}
\citet{§1, pp. 2-4, Eqs. (3), (8)}{weinberg2020massless}.}

\p{The integer residue in \ref{fdip-017C} labels a particular
finite rotation on a grid field. Its input #{\xi} is a spectral
value on the fiber of the centered grid symbol. Neither quantity
defines a one-particle helicity.}

\p{The behavior of site symmetries under restriction is treated in [Refining the grid](fdip-01BN).}
